{
  "analysis": "Round 1 proved small-scale pasting (0.3-0.7x) is the dominant lever (+0.25 inclusion AP), and the miss profile is now clear: nearly all misses in every class are small (inclusion median missed 33.8px vs 90.5px train; low-tertile recall 0.158/0.533/0.455) and low-contrast. The kept plan (25/15/10, 0.3-0.7, same_class, uniform) is this round's control. I test three single-factor extensions: (C1) push inclusion scale to the floor 0.3-0.5x (~27-45px, matching the missed band) while keeping patches/scratches at the proven 0.3-0.7; (C2) keep scale but switch to prefer_small source selection for all classes, testing whether source size (independent of scale) shifts the synthetic size distribution toward misses; (C3) shift allocation to 30/10/10 at the proven small scale, testing whether more inclusion data helps now that scale is fixed (round 1 showed allocation alone was weak only at normal scale). All keep same_class (any_class hurt patches), uniform overlap 0.15, and all sources.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion misses concentrate at ~34px (low-tertile recall 0.158, n=146); shrinking pastes to 0.3-0.5x source size (~27-45px) will raise inclusion AP50 and small-tertile recall beyond the 0.3-0.7 band. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 clearly exceeds 0.386 and mAP50 > 0.60 vs control; if inclusion AP drops, the floor is too small/noisy and I stop at 0.3-0.7.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 15, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.5, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Selecting smaller source defects (prefer_small) shifts the synthetic size distribution toward the missed band independently of the scale multiplier, improving small-tertile recall across all three classes (all show the same small-miss pattern). Confirmed if mAP50 > 0.60 with inclusion AP50 up and patches/scratches AP50 not below control; if it ties C1, scale is the better smallness lever.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 15, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "With small scale proven, allocation becomes the active lever again: 5 extra inclusion images (30 vs 25) at the expense of patches (15->10, already strongest at 0.76) will raise inclusion AP50 without materially hurting patches. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 rises and patches AP50 stays >= ~0.74; if patches drop >0.03, allocation is not worth the trade and I keep 25/15/10 in round 3.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "C4 control (repeat 25/15/10 @ 0.3-0.7) wins at mAP50 0.6116, ahead of C1 (0.599) by >0.005 so no tie-break. C1's hypothesis was refuted: shrinking inclusion to 0.3-0.5 dropped inclusion AP50 to 0.290 (-0.096 vs before, -0.061 vs control) while patches/scratches ticked up — so 0.3-0.7 is the floor, not 0.3-0.5. C2 (prefer_small, mAP 0.587) and C3 (30/10/10, inclusion 0.344) both underperformed the control, confirming source-size and extra allocation are not the inclusion lever. Inclusion AP is noisy (~0.34-0.39) and stuck; the proven recipe is 0.3-0.7 / same_class / uniform, now with scratches (0.712) and patches (0.772) carrying the mAP.",
  "choice": 4,
  "lesson": "Round 2: 0.3-0.7 is inclusion's scale sweet spot — pushing to 0.3-0.5 clearly HURT inclusion (0.290 vs 0.352 control), and both prefer_small sources (mAP 0.587) and extra inclusion allocation 30/10/10 (inclusion 0.344) failed to move it, so scale-down, source-size, and allocation are all exhausted for inclusion. The control (repeat 25/15/10 @ 0.3-0.7, same_class, uniform) is the best at mAP 0.6116, led by scratches 0.712 / patches 0.772 with inclusion stuck ~0.35. For the final round, keep 0.3-0.7/same_class/uniform as the floor (the lab control will repeat it) and only test small evidence-backed nudges (e.g. a touch more scale headroom 0.3-0.8 or higher max_overlap for inclusion), not untested extremes."
}
